<h2 id="hidden">7. The Extras That Push the Real Cost Up</h2>

<p>The box price is not the real price. Every kit here arrives short of something. Here is what I actually had to buy on top.</p>

<p>Pay attention to this number: the light. Every kit under $200 ships with a light between 20 and 30 watts. That grows herbs, and that is it. If a 12-pod system means lettuce and mint, the $30 you saved on the kit goes straight into a <a href="/posts/best-led-grow-lights-for-beginners">real grow light</a> within a month, because the bundled one is the first thing you outgrow.</p>

<p>Then there is the medium. Kits arrive with foam pods or a few plugs, and once they are spent, you are buying again. A bag of <a href="/posts/hydroponic-growing-medium">rockwool cubes or clay pebbles</a> runs $12 to $30, and the choice barely changes your harvest, so buy the cheap bag. You will also want a <a href="/posts/best-ph-meter-for-hydroponics">pH pen</a> around $15 to $25 and a two-part <a href="/posts/hydroponic-nutrient-guide">nutrient kit</a> for $18 to $25, because the sample packs kits ship with last two weeks.</p>

<p>Real first-year cost, from my spreadsheet: the iDOO 12-pod was $70, plus $19 for nutrients, $16 for perlite, and a $22 pH pen. That is $127 for the first year, and $36 of it is stuff the box never included.</p>

<h2 id="mistakes">8. Three Things That Went Wrong in My First Month</h2>

<p>I lost one whole lettuce batch in my first month, and it was never the kit's fault. It was the three mistakes below, in order.</p>

<p><strong>One: I filled the reservoir to the brim.</strong> The water sat an inch above the net pots, so the roots drowned in stale water with no air gap. The fix was leaving a half-inch pocket of air under the pot, and that is more honestly the whole lesson: <a href="/posts/hydroponic-growing-medium">the water line</a> is the most repeated beginner failure, and it is not about the medium.</p>

<p><strong>Two: I never measured pH.</strong> The rockwool I started seeds in ran the reservoir to 7.6, and lettuce stalls above 6.5. Ten minutes with a $22 pen on day one would have saved the whole tray.</p>

<p><strong>Three: I used cold tap water straight from the sink.</strong> The first fill shocked the roots and every seedling lagged for two weeks. I started letting the water sit in the jug overnight so it reached room temperature, and the next batch took off.</p>

<p>All three were free to fix. That is the thing nobody puts in the box: the errors are cheap, but nobody warns you they are coming.</p>

<h2>Which one should you buy?</h2>

<p>If your budget is under $100 and you want something that works out of the box, get the <strong>iDOO 12-pod</strong>. It's the best bang for your buck.</p>

<p>If you're willing to spend 15 minutes assembling parts, build the <strong>DIY DWC bucket</strong>. It's cheaper and better than any kit.</p>

<p>If you want to grow tomatoes and peppers from day one, get the <strong>Spider Farmer 4-in-1</strong>. The 100W light makes all the difference.</p>

<p>If you just want basil on your counter and don't want to think about it, get the <strong>AeroGarden Harvest</strong>. It's overpriced but it works.</p>

